infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title When batching pays: speedup → N vs speedup → 1
  desc Per-item cost = fixed F + variable V·n; the ratio of F to V·n decides whether a batch is a huge win or pointless
  compares
    - label Fixed cost dominates (F > V·n)
      children
        - label Small payloads — huge win
          desc logs, metrics, key lookups: per-call overhead is the whole story
        - label Speedup → N
          desc batch 100 items → up to ~100×; the N×F term swamps everything
        - label Network: 1KB, 50µs RTT, batch 100
          desc 5ms of round-trips → ~150µs ≈ 33× faster
        - label Redis pipelining: 10,000 PINGs
          desc 1.185s unpipelined → 0.250s ≈ 5×
    - label Variable cost dominates (F < V·n)
      children
        - label Large payloads — no gain
          desc video chunks, big uploads: you already pay mostly for bytes
        - label Speedup → 1
          desc nothing to amortize; the V·N·n term swamps everything
        - label 4KB at 40µs/KB vs 50µs RTT
          desc V·n = 160µs > F = 50µs → batching only adds latency
        - label Break-even per item: F = V·n
          desc below it batching pays; above it batching is pointless
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
